# The vendored Qt recipes and helpers are .ps1 files that RUN ON WINDOWS.
# Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads a .ps1 saved as UTF-8 *without* BOM as ANSI
# (cp1252), and any non-ASCII byte sequence then decodes into a typographic
# quote that the parser accepts as a string delimiter: the script fails to load
# with "The string is missing the terminator". The files in the repo are ASCII
# + BOM + CRLF on purpose; this keeps them that way on a checkout made from
# Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which would otherwise strip the CRLF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# The vendored Qt trees are BUILD OUTPUT, not source we edit: a prefix layout of
# DLLs, import libs, headers and the .conf/.prl files that `qmake` and
# `windeployqt` parse on Windows. Marking them -text keeps git from rewriting
# the line endings of those .conf/.prl on a checkout made from Windows with
# core.autocrlf=true, which qmake would then fail to parse. Same treatment as
# the vendored FFmpeg tree.
third_party/qt-win64/** -text
third_party/qt-win32/** -text
third_party/ffmpeg-win32/** -text
